{
 "id": "teacher-notifications",
 "title": "Teacher Notifications",
 "pattern": "strings",
 "level": "medium",
 "statement": "<p>A school admin API lets teachers register students, suspend students, and send notifications. When a teacher sends a notification, the API must work out who receives it.</p><p>A student receives the notification if <b>both</b> of these hold:</p><ul><li>the student is <b>not suspended</b>, and</li><li>the student is <b>registered</b> to that teacher, <b>or</b> is <b>@mentioned</b> in the notification text.</li></ul><p>An @mention is <code>@</code> followed by an email, at the start of the text or right after whitespace (a space or a newline), for example <code>@studentagnes@gmail.com</code>. A mentioned student counts even if no teacher has registered them. The email is a local part (letters, digits, <code>. _ + -</code>), then <code>@</code>, then a domain of two or more parts joined by dots (each part letters, digits, <code>_</code> or <code>-</code>). Take the longest email that fits, so a trailing <code>.</code>, <code>,</code> or <code>!</code> is not part of it. An email without the leading <code>@</code> is not a mention.</p><p>Write <code>def solution(registrations, suspended, teacher, notification)</code>. <code>registrations</code> is a list of <code>[teacher_email, student_email]</code> pairs, <code>suspended</code> is a list of student emails, <code>teacher</code> is the sender's email and <code>notification</code> is the text. Return the recipients' emails as a list, sorted, with no duplicates.</p>",
 "examples": [
  {
   "input": "registrations = [[\"teacherken@gmail.com\", \"studentjon@gmail.com\"], [\"teacherken@gmail.com\", \"studenthon@gmail.com\"]], suspended = [], teacher = \"teacherken@gmail.com\", notification = \"Hello students! @studentagnes@gmail.com @studentmiche@gmail.com\"",
   "output": "['studentagnes@gmail.com', 'studenthon@gmail.com', 'studentjon@gmail.com', 'studentmiche@gmail.com']",
   "why": "Two registered students plus two mentioned ones. Agnes and Miche are not registered to anyone, but a mention is enough."
  },
  {
   "input": "registrations = [[\"teacherken@gmail.com\", \"studentbob@gmail.com\"], [\"teacherjoe@gmail.com\", \"studentmary@gmail.com\"]], suspended = [], teacher = \"teacherken@gmail.com\", notification = \"Hey everybody\"",
   "output": "['studentbob@gmail.com']",
   "why": "No mentions, so only Ken's own student. Mary belongs to Joe."
  },
  {
   "input": "registrations = [[\"teacherken@gmail.com\", \"studentbob@gmail.com\"], [\"teacherken@gmail.com\", \"studentmary@gmail.com\"]], suspended = [\"studentmary@gmail.com\", \"studentagnes@gmail.com\"], teacher = \"teacherken@gmail.com\", notification = \"Exam moved @studentagnes@gmail.com\"",
   "output": "['studentbob@gmail.com']",
   "why": "Mary is registered and Agnes is mentioned, but both are suspended. Suspension beats both rules."
  }
 ],
 "constraints": [
  "0 &le; len(registrations) &le; 20,000",
  "0 &le; len(suspended) &le; 20,000",
  "0 &le; len(notification) &le; 100,000 characters",
  "emails use letters, digits and <code>. _ + -</code>; compare them exactly (case-sensitive)",
  "the same <code>[teacher, student]</code> pair may appear more than once"
 ],
 "starter": "def solution(registrations, suspended, teacher, notification):\n    # write your code here\n    pass\n",
 "think": [
  {
   "step": "Understand",
   "q": "Say the rule as one boolean expression.",
   "a": "<code>not suspended and (registered_to_teacher or mentioned)</code>. Two sources of candidates (the registration table and the text), one filter (suspension), then sort and de-duplicate."
  },
  {
   "step": "Small example by hand",
   "q": "Ken has Jon. Text is <code>\"Hi @studentjon@gmail.com and @studentamy@gmail.com\"</code>. Amy is suspended.",
   "a": "Candidates: Jon (registered), Jon again and Amy (mentioned). De-duplicate: Jon, Amy. Drop suspended Amy. Answer <code>[\"studentjon@gmail.com\"]</code>."
  },
  {
   "step": "Naive idea",
   "q": "What is the first version you would write?",
   "a": "Split the text on spaces, keep tokens starting with <code>@</code>, and use lists for everything. It works on the happy path, but <code>x in list</code> is O(n), so 20,000 candidates against 20,000 suspended students is 400 million comparisons. Punctuation like <code>@a@x.com,</code> also needs special handling."
  },
  {
   "step": "Which pattern?",
   "q": "Clue words?",
   "a": "\"@mentioned\" &rarr; <b>string parsing with a regex</b>. \"not suspended\", \"no duplicates\" &rarr; <b>sets</b> for O(1) membership and free de-duplication. A regex states exactly what a mention is, instead of a pile of <code>strip</code> calls."
  },
  {
   "step": "Plan in plain steps",
   "q": "Write the steps.",
   "a": "<ol><li>Turn <code>suspended</code> into a set.</li><li>Collect the teacher's students into a set.</li><li>Add every regex match from the text.</li><li>Filter out suspended emails and return <code>sorted(...)</code>.</li></ol>"
  },
  {
   "step": "Edge cases to test",
   "q": "What could break?",
   "a": "A student both registered and mentioned (appears once). A mention followed by punctuation. A plain email without the leading <code>@</code> (not a mention). A teacher with no students. Everyone suspended. Empty text."
  }
 ],
 "hints": [
  "Use sets: one for the suspended students, one for the recipients. Adding the same email twice to a set is harmless.",
  "Write the mention regex in two parts: <code>(?:^|\\s)@</code> for \"start or after whitespace, then @\", and a capture group for the email: <code>([\\w.+-]+@[\\w-]+(?:\\.[\\w-]+)+)</code>.",
  "<code>re.findall</code> with one capture group returns just the captured emails. Then <code>return sorted(s for s in recipients if s not in blocked)</code>.",
  "Browser note: this page runs Python in the browser (Brython), whose <code>re</code> mishandles <code>(?:^|\\s)</code> inside a group. Write <code>(?&lt;!\\S)@</code> (\"an @ not preceded by a non-space\") instead; it means the same and works everywhere."
 ],
 "solution": "import re\n\n# \"@\" at the start or after whitespace ((?<!\\S) means \"not right after a non-space\"), then an email: local@domain.tld\nMENTION = re.compile(r'(?<!\\S)@([\\w.+-]+@[\\w-]+(?:\\.[\\w-]+)+)')\n\ndef solution(registrations, suspended, teacher, notification):\n    blocked = set(suspended)                       # O(1) \"is suspended?\" checks\n    # students registered to this teacher\n    recipients = {s for t, s in registrations if t == teacher}\n    # plus everyone @mentioned, known or not\n    recipients.update(MENTION.findall(notification))\n    # drop suspended students; the set already removed duplicates\n    return sorted(s for s in recipients if s not in blocked)\n",
 "brute": "def solution(registrations, suspended, teacher, notification):\n    # Token by token, no regex, lists instead of sets (slow membership checks)\n    candidates = []\n    for t, s in registrations:\n        if t == teacher:\n            candidates.append(s)\n    for token in notification.split():\n        if token.startswith('@') and '@' in token[1:]:\n            candidates.append(token[1:].rstrip('.,!?;:'))\n    result = []\n    for s in candidates:\n        if s not in suspended and s not in result:\n            result.append(s)\n    result.sort()\n    return result\n",
 "explain": "<p>Treat it as two inputs feeding one set, then one filter.</p><ul><li>Registered to <code>teacherken</code>: <code>{studentjon, studenthon}</code>.</li><li>Mentions in <code>\"Hello students! @studentagnes@gmail.com @studentmiche@gmail.com\"</code>: the regex finds <code>studentagnes@gmail.com</code> and <code>studentmiche@gmail.com</code>. <code>\"students!\"</code> has no <code>@</code>, so it is ignored.</li><li>Union: 4 emails. None suspended. Sorted: agnes, hon, jon, miche.</li></ul><p>Why a regex: the email part <code>[\\w-]+(?:\\.[\\w-]+)+</code> must end on a letter or digit, so <code>\"@studentjon@gmail.com.\"</code> yields <code>studentjon@gmail.com</code> without the full stop. In the real take-home this logic sits behind <code>POST /api/retrievefornotifications</code>; keep it in a pure function like this one so you can unit-test it without a database.</p>",
 "complexity": "O(R + S + L + K log K) time for R registrations, S suspended, text length L and K recipients; O(R + S) extra space",
